================================================================================
  SHARED vs DEDICATED BISR  -  REPAIR VERIFICATION
================================================================================
  Memories  : 2 SRAMs, 256 rows x 8 bits each
  Dedicated : 2 BISR controllers (one per SRAM)
              2 local spare rows per SRAM                    -> 4 spare rows
  Shared    : 1 BISR controller(s), 2 SRAMs per controller
              1 local spare row per SRAM + 2 global spare rows
              per controller (borrowable by any of its SRAMs) -> 4 spare rows
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Each test : inject faults -> run BISR on both designs -> compare with the
              expected result -> if repaired, write/read every row
  Results   : PASS         all faulty rows replaced and VERIFY clean
              UNREPAIRABLE not enough spare rows (or fault table full)
              VERIFY FAIL  an assigned spare row is itself faulty
              [OK]         design result = expected result
              L / G        local / global spare rows used (shared)
================================================================================

PART 1 - DIRECTED TESTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

T1  No faults
    Shows  : a fault-free system passes and uses no spares
    Faults : none
      Dedicated : PASS          spares used  0/ 4            expected PASS                [OK]
      Shared    : PASS          spares used  0/ 4 (L0 + G0)  expected PASS                [OK]
      Read/write of all 256 rows after repair : dedicated OK | shared OK

T2  One faulty row in every SRAM
    Shows  : each SRAM repairs with its own local spare
    Faults : SRAM0  row  10  bit 0  stuck-at-0     
             SRAM1  row  47  bit 1  stuck-at-1     
      Dedicated : PASS          spares used  2/ 4            expected PASS                [OK]
      Shared    : PASS          spares used  2/ 4 (L2 + G0)  expected PASS                [OK]
      Read/write of all 256 rows after repair : dedicated OK | shared OK

T3  Several bad bits in one row
    Shows  : bad bits in the same row are merged and use ONE spare row
    Faults : SRAM0  row  50  bit 0  stuck-at-0     
             SRAM0  row  50  bit 1  stuck-at-1     
             SRAM0  row  50  bit 6  0->1 transition
      Dedicated : PASS          spares used  1/ 4            expected PASS                [OK]
      Shared    : PASS          spares used  1/ 4 (L1 + G0)  expected PASS                [OK]
      Read/write of all 256 rows after repair : dedicated OK | shared OK

T4  Three faulty rows in SRAM0
    Shows  : dedicated runs out (2 spares); shared borrows 2 global spares
    Faults : SRAM0  row   5  bit 0  stuck-at-0     
             SRAM0  row 100  bit 4  0->1 transition
             SRAM0  row 250  bit 6  1->0 transition
      Dedicated : UNREPAIRABLE  spares used  2/ 4            expected UNREPAIRABLE        [OK]
                  why: SRAM0 has 3 faulty rows but only 2 spare rows
      Shared    : PASS          spares used  3/ 4 (L1 + G2)  expected PASS                [OK]
      Read/write of all 256 rows after repair : dedicated - | shared OK

T5  Two faulty rows in SRAM0 and two in SRAM1
    Shows  : both repair: dedicated uses 2+2 local, shared 2 local + 2 global
    Faults : SRAM0  row   7  bit 2  stuck-at-0     
             SRAM0  row   9  bit 5  stuck-at-1     
             SRAM1  row   7  bit 3  1->0 transition
             SRAM1  row   9  bit 4  0->1 transition
      Dedicated : PASS          spares used  4/ 4            expected PASS                [OK]
      Shared    : PASS          spares used  4/ 4 (L2 + G2)  expected PASS                [OK]
      Read/write of all 256 rows after repair : dedicated OK | shared OK

T6  Three faulty rows in the LAST SRAM
    Shows  : the global pool can be borrowed by any SRAM of its controller
    Faults : SRAM1  row   3  bit 0  stuck-at-1     
             SRAM1  row  14  bit 1  stuck-at-1     
             SRAM1  row  25  bit 2  stuck-at-1     
      Dedicated : UNREPAIRABLE  spares used  2/ 4            expected UNREPAIRABLE        [OK]
                  why: SRAM1 has 3 faulty rows but only 2 spare rows
      Shared    : PASS          spares used  3/ 4 (L1 + G2)  expected PASS                [OK]
      Read/write of all 256 rows after repair : dedicated - | shared OK

T7  More faulty rows in SRAM0 than any design can cover
    Shows  : limit: 1 local + whole pool for shared, 2 local for dedicated
    Faults : SRAM0  row  20  bit 0  stuck-at-0     
             SRAM0  row  33  bit 1  stuck-at-1     
             SRAM0  row  46  bit 2  0->1 transition
             SRAM0  row  59  bit 3  1->0 transition
      Dedicated : UNREPAIRABLE  spares used  2/ 4            expected UNREPAIRABLE        [OK]
                  why: SRAM0 has 4 faulty rows but only 2 spare rows
      Shared    : UNREPAIRABLE  spares used  3/ 4 (L1 + G2)  expected UNREPAIRABLE        [OK]
                  why: controller 0: 3 rows need a global spare, pool has 2

T8  Faulty spare row
    Shows  : a defective spare is caught by the VERIFY pass in both designs
    Faults : SRAM0  row  20  bit 0  stuck-at-1     
             SRAM0  LOCAL SPARE ROW  bit 2  stuck-at-0     
      Dedicated : VERIFY FAIL   spares used  1/ 4            expected VERIFY FAIL         [OK]
                  why: spare row given to SRAM0 is itself faulty (caught by VERIFY)
      Shared    : VERIFY FAIL   spares used  1/ 4 (L1 + G0)  expected VERIFY FAIL         [OK]
                  why: spare row given to SRAM0 is itself faulty (caught by VERIFY)

